WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.310
Ten years in the making, Avengers: Infinity
War is the culmination of all of Marvel's

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.320
awesome efforts on the big screen, marking
the end of many things… and very likely

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.860
the beginning of even more things.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.420
And even though Infinity War is pretty focused
on the here and now rather than the deeper

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.570
secrets hidden within the pages of Marvel
Comics, this movie didn't disappoint True

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.200
Believers.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:25.220
In fact, its many true-to-comics scenes and
unexpected callbacks stole the show.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.619
Here's a spoiler-filled look at a few of the
Easter eggs you may have missed in Avengers:

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.340
Infinity War.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.900
And one more time, in case you missed it:
massive spoilers ahead.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.240
"Mantis!  Look out!"

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.760
The Mark L

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.320
Tony Stark is probably a few hundred armors
deep when it comes to his comics counterpart,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.120
but in the MCU, he's just reached armor number
50: the Mark L. This time around, he's dropped

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.220
the complicated suit-up gantry, the fancy
suitcase, and even the legion of armors that

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.520
can just fly around and protect him from his
enemies.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.280
Tony's newest armor, which he wears in a small
box on his chest, is all about portability

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.000
and convenience, and it's pulled directly
from a couple of comics sources.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:14.020
Iron Man's S.K.I.N. armor debuted in 2001,
and was mostly housed in a small, self-contained

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.200
unit that could release a simple, liquefied
form of his suit around his whole body instantly.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.920
Later, Iron Man's Bleeding Edge armor would
do something similar, except it formed a complete

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:27.310
suit of armor… and it was housed in the
hollows of Tony's bones, rather than in an

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.160
oversized locket.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.420
Kind of gross, but anything to not have to
wear gaudy, glowing jewelry, right?

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.980
A few extra legs

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.720
We've been seeing previews of Spider-Man in
his fancy new "Iron Spider" armor for months

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.040
now, after he'd first rejected it at the end
of Spider-Man Homecoming.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.600
It's not too far into the movie before he
gets his first feel for the slick new suit,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.900
and immediately stows away on the trip to
Titan, much to Tony's annoyance.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.200
But unless you caught one very specific statue
design that was leaked way too early, nobody

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.360
really expected the suit to sprout a bunch
of extra legs and save the day.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.560
That is, unless you're familiar with the Iron
Spider armor in the comics, which has that

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.100
exact feature.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.860
While it initially seemed too outlandish for
the big screen, those extra appendages definitely

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:12.580
came in handy.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.420
And when Iron Man can combine his feet to
make one giant rocket-foot, a couple of extra

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.660
spider-legs isn't really that weird anymore.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:21.760
Stormbreaker

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.000
Rest in pieces, Mjolnir.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.580
It was pretty much inevitable that the guy
known for swinging a mystical weapon would

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.120
end up getting his hands on another one pretty
quickly after his iconic hammer was destroyed

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:32.620
in Ragnarok.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:33.320
"Hammer?"

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:38.140
It was quite unique.  It was made from this special metal from the heart of a dying star and

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.760
when I spun it around really really fast it gave me the ability to fly.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:41.860
You flew a hammer?"

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.880
Thor's new accessory goes by the name of Stormbreaker.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.480
Loyal comics readers, of course, know that
name's already been taken by Beta Ray Bill's

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:53.900
weapon, marking the second time that the powerful,
Thor-like space-horse has been referenced

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:54.820
in the MCU.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.579
Bill is probably out there in the galaxy somewhere,
assuming he wasn't reduced to dust in Thanos'

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.580
great culling of the universe, anyway, and
Stormbreaker is ready for his arrival.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.860
Can his first full Marvel Cinematic Universe
appearance be too far behind?

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:09.900
Fear Itself

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.180
Speaking of Asgardian allies, Thor seems to
have pals everywhere.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.240
"Daryl is an average sorta everyday guy, so
it works out well."

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.299
Marvel is once again borrowing parts of their
own previously published comics for their

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:27.849
blockbuster movies, having previously made
significant tweaks to Civil War and Planet

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:29.420
Hulk to get them to the big screen.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.020
This time, Fear Itself plays a minor role.

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:36.620
Sure, in Fear Itself, it was Tony Stark visiting
the cosmic dwarves to make weapons for the

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.769
Avengers to fight the Serpent… but the general
idea is the same: Thor visits the dwarf Eitri

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:45.320
to get a new, Thanos-smashing weapon, for
better or worse.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.700
Mostly worse.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.840
A tour of the galaxy

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.560
Nidavellir and Vormir
aren't just really confusing crossword puzzle

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.360
answers, they're actual places in the Marvel
Comics universe.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:04:00.600
The Asgardian arm of Marvel is pretty much
just a wild riff on actual Norse mythology

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:05.769
with slightly more preposterous muscles, so
Nidavellir has real sources in actual historical

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:10.460
documents dating back to the year 1270, which
place it as the home of the dwarves.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.380
No big questions there; Marvel just uses it
pretty much like they found it.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.320
Vormir, on the other hand, has never been
that important in Marvel continuity.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:22.600
In the comics, it's the home of a bunch of
16-foot tall, dragon-like aliens who feed

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:23.340
on planets.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.880
When Thanos and Gamora arrive there in Infinity
War looking for the Soul Stone, there aren't

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.040
any dragons to be found.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.020
There really isn't much of anything.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.790
The significance ends there; it's just a pearl
buried deep, deep in Avengers lore.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.960
But what they did find on Vormir is even more
surprising than what they didn't…

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.420
Return of the Skull

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.430
In a move almost nobody saw coming, First
Avenger villain Red Skull re-appeared in the

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:51.910
MCU as the cosmic guardian of the Soul Stone,
having paid the ultimate price for his search

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:52.960
for cosmic power.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.300
He's never actually referred to as Red Skull,
but he speaks of his quest for the Stones

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.460
and how it led him to his imprisonment on
Vormir.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.600
Also, he has a red skull.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.100
It's pretty obvious.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:07.940
Fans familiar with Thanos' comic book quest
to make the physical embodiment of Death fall

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.880
in love with him probably also noticed the
similarities between Red Skull's new all-black,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.640
ghoulish form and Marvel's personification
of Death.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.440
It's a great twist on Thanos' comic relationships,
and seeing it play out like this here pretty

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.300
much guarantees we'll never have to see a
Thanos/Death makeout scene.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:25.960
Everyone wins.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:27.880
Cut to ribbons

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.740
Thanos' deal is generally much more about
destruction than creation, but that doesn't

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:37.470
mean he's not one heck of a creative guy,
defusing situations by turning laser blasts

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.740
into bubbles and building giant altars to
girls who don't like him.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.440
In an effort to show off for Death during
Infinity Gauntlet, Thanos turns Nebula into

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.720
an unraveling ribbon, and his own brother
Starfox into a disjointed stack of blocks.

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:54.600
The details are a little different in the
movie, but he does the same thing to Mantis

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.100
and Drax on Knowwhere.

00:05:56.509 --> 00:06:01.220
The sentiment is there, however: Thanos is
powerful, weird, and ready to turn anyone

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.780
into crafting materials at a moment's notice.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.420
Morgan Stark

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.060
When you're a superhero, there's a 95 percent
chance that your relatives will either be

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.920
other superheroes or villains that you'll
one day have to face.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.139
Even when you're apparently adopted, like
Tony Stark, your secret brothers and cousins

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.120
will come along and derail your life.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:23.300
Such is the case with Morgan Stark, Tony's
rarely-mentioned cousin.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.020
Morgan is an occasional hassle to Tony, a
guy involved in mob business who sometimes

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.340
attempts to take over Stark Industries with
his super-team, the Stockpile.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.760
And like Iron Man, he throws on a high-tech
costume, but calls himself Brass.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.260
When you name yourself after the metal that
tubas are made from, you're already off to

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:41.360
a pretty crappy start.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:45.780
So, while it seems like Tony doesn't even
really have any extended family to worry about

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.460
in the MCU, it's just a little alarming that
Tony wants to name his unborn kid Morgan.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.539
It's just a little like Batman naming his
kid "Joker."

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:54.620
Don't do it.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.660
Cauldron of the Cosmos

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.360
Doctor Strange introduced audiences to an
array of what the mystical side of the MCU

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.409
might have to offer: living capes, the Eye
of Agamotto, and the Wand of Watoomb are among

00:07:06.409 --> 00:07:08.440
the treasures housed in the New York Sanctum.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.500
Infinity War also confirms the presence of
the Cauldron of the Cosmos:

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.600
"He can destroy life on a scale hitherto undreamt
of?"

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.300
"Did you seriously just say 'hitherto undreamt
of?'"

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.280
"Are you seriously leaning on the Cauldron
of the Cosmos?"

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.300
"Is that what that is?"

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.340
The cauldron is basically Dr. Strange's time
TV, allowing him to see into whatever era

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:27.440
he wants to muck around in.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:33.280
In Marvel Team-Up #112, he uses it to check
out a scene 20,000 years in the past, so it

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.800
doesn't really seem to have any limits.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:39.860
Why he keeps it as a decoration in his stairwell
is anyone's guess, but he's obviously not

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:42.140
happy when Tony Stark uses it to lean on.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:47.060
And while it's not 100 percent confirmed,
those bright red magical ropes that Dr. Strange

00:07:47.069 --> 00:07:51.939
was using to restrain Thanos on Titan sure
looked like the Crimson Bands of Cyttorak,

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.480
one of Strange's most iconic incantations.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:56.420
Nevernude

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:01.870
Infinity War was directed by the Russo brothers,
whose previous credits include directing multiple

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.720
episodes of the cult hit comedy show Arrested
Development.

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:09.680
The bros hid one of Arrested Development's
iconic vehicles, the stair car, in the airport

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.140
battle scene in Civil War.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.639
They're back again with the Easter eggs, this
time dropping one of the show's actual characters

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:17.720
into the Collector's assemblage of treasures.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.020
It's a weirdly complicated reference, but
if you check out the Collector's cases just

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:27.300
as the scene begins, you'll spot a bald, blue
man in cutoff shorts lounging in the background.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:28.340
Look familiar?

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:29.600
"You haven't auditioned yet?"

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.740
"Oh no, no, no I'm not in the group yet.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.460
I'm afraid I just blue myself."

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.519
It's absolutely ridiculous, but we can see
why the Collector would want him for his collection.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:40.020
He's one of a kind.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.580
Stan the Man

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:48.440
We're still not completely sure if Marvel
Comics mastermind Stan Lee is the MCU's mailman,

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.980
security guard, cosmic watcher, or what…
but we do know that in the case of Infinity

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.740
War, he's a bus driver for a bunch of punk
kids.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:00.340
The film gets Stan's cameo out of the way
super early, just as Peter Parker's senses

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:03.300
get all tingly about the approaching donut
of doom.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.460
A Marvel-ous ending

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.370
Infinity War skips the mid-credits scene,
pretty much because there's nothing left to

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:13.640
say after Thanos does his Thanos thing and
turns half of the universe's living creatures

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:14.880
into frosted flakes.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.420
But in the film's post-credits scene, Nick
Fury puts out a last-second SOS to a mysterious

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.920
person who'll definitely have a huge role
in the Avengers sequel.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.180
It may not be obvious to anyone who isn't
up on their superhero iconography, but it's

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:33.040
a teaser for the upcoming Captain Marvel movie,
scheduled for a 2019 release date.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.380
Captain Marvel stars Brie Larson as Carol
Danvers, a pilot who gets incredible abilities.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:41.480
We're talking Thor levels of power, or maybe
more.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.360
No wonder MODOK develops a massive crush on
her.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:48.100
Also, can we finally get MODOK in the MCU,
please?

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:52.240
"Tis some kind of that is a very big head!"

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.220
Thanks for watching!

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:56.780
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.560
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

